<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Hooke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1635">1635</a>–<a href="/years/1703">1703</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Hooke was an English polymath, born at Freshwater on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a>, who was active as a physicist, astronomer, geologist, meteorologist and architect. He began as an assistant to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-boyle">Robert Boyle</a>, building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vacuum-pump">vacuum pumps</a> for his experiments, and from 1662 was the first Curator of Experiments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>. His book Micrographia (1665), drawn from work with a compound <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/microscope">microscope</a> he designed, coined the term cell. He inferred that gravity obeys an inverse square law, which led to a rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>, and identified the organic origin of fossils. After the Great Fire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1666 he performed more than half of the property line surveys for its reconstruction.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…arranged in 1648 showed that the air has weight, pressing less on a mountain top than at its foot, and Boyle, whose assistant <b>Robert Hooke</b> built him an air pump to study the “spring” of the air, published in 1662 the law of gases that bears his name.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The Principia grew out of a conversation in January 1684, when Edmond Halley, Christopher Wren and <b>Robert Hooke</b> discussed planetary motion.</blockquote><a href="/work/principia">Principia · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
